# Cloudflare Pages routing rules. First match wins; requests that match a real static file are
# served directly and never reach these.
#
# THE PROBLEM. Pages answers anything it cannot match with the SPA fallback -- index.html, 200,
# text/html. That is correct for routes (/login and /about are not files; the client router owns
# them) and actively harmful for assets. A missing /assets/*.js came back as 200 text/html, so the
# browser reported "Expected a JavaScript-or-Wasm module script but the server responded with a
# MIME type of text/html" rather than a plain 404. Measured on production before this rule existed:
#
#   /assets/index-DSdlyTNG.js   200  application/javascript   (exists)
#   /assets/does-not-exist.js   200  text/html                (fallback -- the bug)
#
# A 404 is the honest answer, and it is the one Vite's own preload-error handling and every
# monitoring tool already understand.
#
# THE CATCH-ALL IS REQUIRED. It was omitted in the first version of this file, on the reasoning
# that Pages' built-in SPA fallback already served routes and a redundant rule could only add risk.
# That was wrong, and the PR preview proved it: adding 404.html for the rule above ALSO replaces the
# SPA fallback, because Pages serves a 404.html for unmatched paths in preference to index.html.
# Every client-side route broke at once --
#
#   /login  404    /about  404    /dashboard  404
#
# -- which is the whole site, not a corner of it. The lesson is narrow and worth keeping: on Pages,
# introducing a 404.html is a routing change, not just a nicer error page.
#
# So routes are restored explicitly below. Ordering is what makes both rules hold: first match wins,
# so /assets/* is answered with a 404 and everything else falls to index.html for the client router.
#
# This does NOT intercept real assets, and that is measured rather than assumed -- on the same
# preview, with the /assets/* rule already active, an existing bundle still returned
# 200 application/javascript. Pages serves a matching static file before it consults this file at
# all, so these rules only ever see requests that matched nothing on disk.
/assets/* /404.html 404
/* /index.html 200
